# Market Failure and the Role of Government

> Markets are magnificent coordination machines — when their prices
> carry the full truth. But some costs hide from prices, some goods
> invite free-riders, some markets tip into monopoly. When prices
> lie, the invisible hand pushes the wrong way, and that is the
> precise boundary where economics hands the reins to government.

## 1. The Benchmark: Why Markets Usually Work

In a perfectly competitive market, price equals marginal cost, every
mutually beneficial trade happens, and the outcome is **Pareto
efficient** — no one can be made better off without making someone
worse off. All of that rests on quiet assumptions: prices reflect all
costs and benefits, property rights are complete, information is
symmetric, and no single buyer or seller can bend the price.

**Market failure** is the name for the systematic breakdown of these
assumptions. Five classic species follow.

## 2. Externalities: Costs and Benefits That Escape the Price

[definition]
An **externality** is a cost or benefit of an economic activity that
falls on third parties and is not reflected in the market price.
Negative externalities (pollution) make private cost smaller than
**social cost**; positive externalities (vaccination, education) make
private benefit smaller than social benefit.
[/definition]

With a negative externality, the market produces **too much**:
private actors compare their own cost with the price, ignoring the
harm dumped on others. The gap is corrected by a **Pigouvian tax**
set equal to the marginal external cost — raising private cost to
social cost and shrinking output to the social optimum. Positive
externalities call for the mirror policy: subsidies that lower the
private price and expand output.

**Worked example.** A factory's each tonne of steel costs it ₹30,000
to make but dumps ₹10,000 of damage on downstream villages. Social
cost = ₹40,000. A tax of ₹10,000 per tonne makes the factory's books
tell the social truth, and the highest-cost, dirtiest tonnes
disappear from production.

**Coase theorem:** if property rights are clear and transaction costs
negligible, private bargaining can internalise the externality
whatever the initial assignment of rights. In practice, transaction
costs, many victims and missing property rights are the norm — which
is why smog is bargained about in parliaments, not between a million
commuters.

## 3. Public Goods: Non-rival, Non-excludable

[definition]
A **public good** is **non-rival** (one person's consumption does not
diminish another's) and **non-excludable** (no one can be prevented
from consuming it): national defence, street lighting, lighthouses,
clean air. Because no one can be charged, private markets
under-provide them — the **free-rider problem**.
[/definition]

| | Rival | Non-rival |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Excludable** | private good (bread) | club good (streaming, toll roads) |
| **Non-excludable** | common resource (fisheries) | public good (defence) |

Common resources are rival but non-excludable — each user ignores the
congestion imposed on others, and the result is overuse: the
**tragedy of the commons**. Remedies: quotas, licenses, property
rights or community management.

## 4. Imperfect Competition and Natural Monopoly

When one or few sellers control price, output is restricted below the
competitive level and price sits above marginal cost — a deadweight
loss. **Natural monopoly** is the special case where economies of
scale are so large that a single firm serves the whole market
cheapest (rail tracks, electricity distribution). Options: public
ownership, or price regulation (marginal-cost pricing needs a
subsidy; average-cost pricing breaks even).

Antitrust (competition law) polices cartels, collusion and abusive
dominance — trust-busting from Standard Oil to modern platform
regulation.

## 5. Information Asymmetry

When one side of a trade knows more than the other, markets misfire:

- **Adverse selection** (hidden type): before the deal. The sickest
  customers buy the most insurance; the market for lemons (Akerlof)
  can unravel entirely as good cars are driven out.
- **Moral hazard** (hidden action): after the deal. The insured drive
  carelessly; bailed-out banks gamble again.

Responses: warranties, deductibles, screening and signalling
(education as a credible signal of ability — Spence), disclosure
regulation, credit bureaus.

## 6. The Government's Toolkit

| Failure | Standard instruments |
| --- | --- |
| Externalities | Pigouvian taxes, cap-and-trade, subsidies |
| Public goods | Direct provision, procurement |
| Common resources | Quotas, licences, property rights |
| Monopoly | Antitrust, regulation, public provision |
| Information gaps | Mandatory disclosure, standards, consumer law |
| Macroeconomic instability | Fiscal and monetary policy |

Meritorious interventions also include **merit goods** (education,
vaccination — under-consumed from the social viewpoint) and
**redistribution**, justified not by efficiency but by society's
preferences for equity — progressive taxation and transfers are the
usual instruments.

## 7. Government Failure: The Mirror Image

Interventions carry their own diseases: information problems (the
regulator knows less than the regulated), **regulatory capture**
(the agency serves the industry it polices), rent-seeking (resources
burnt lobbying for favours), unintended consequences (rent ceilings
shrinking rental supply), and public-choice dynamics (policies timed
to election cycles). The honest comparison is never "imperfect market
versus ideal government" but *both* against each other — which is why
good policy design builds in sunset clauses, evaluation and
competition wherever possible.

## 8. Summary

Markets fail through externalities, public goods, monopoly power and
asymmetric information; each failure has a matching instrument —
taxes and subsidies, public provision, antitrust and regulation,
disclosure and standards. The case for intervention is always
comparative: against a government that can also fail. Choosing the
least-bad instrument, and sizing it to the actual divergence between
private and social cost, is the everyday craft of public economics.
